    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Fume cupboards & DIN EN 14175", "item": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine/lab-container-fume-hood-din-en-14175" }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which standard governs fume cupboards?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The governing standard is the DIN EN 14175 “Fume cupboards” series, which replaced the former DIN 12924-1. It comprises seven parts: Part 1 (Terms), Part 2 (Safety and performance requirements), Part 3 (Type test methods with SF₆ tracer-gas measurement), Part 4 (On-site test methods at the operator), Part 5 (Recommendations for installation and use), Part 6 (VAV fume cupboards with variable air volume) and Part 7 (non-conventional special types). Important: DIN EN 14175 no longer sets fixed guide values for containment or extract volume flow – these are determined by the operator under their own responsibility as part of the risk assessment. In addition, TRGS 526 “Laboratories” and DGUV Information 213-850 apply to laboratory operation, and DIN 1946-7 to room-air technology. Cupboards installed before 2003 remain subject to DIN 12924-1 or 12924-2 as valid at the time of installation." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How high must the face velocity be?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "On older cupboards to DIN 12924 (January 1978 edition) the mean face velocity with the sash open 100 mm must be at least 0.7 m/s – a measured value of only 0.5 m/s is below that and is not sufficient for safe operation. On modern cupboards to DIN EN 14175 there is no blanket velocity value: what matters is the containment demonstrated in the type test; the required extract volume flow is set by the risk assessment. Measurement is made with a thermal or vane anemometer. An important distinction: the frequently cited value of 0.5 m refers to the maximum sash opening height (limiter to Part 2), not to the face velocity." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is containment and how is it tested?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Containment describes the ability of a fume cupboard to keep airborne substances inside the working chamber and not allow them to escape into the breathing zone of the staff. It is tested in the type test to DIN EN 14175-3 with the tracer gas sulphur hexafluoride (SF₆), which is non-toxic but strongly climate-damaging (global warming potential around 30,000 times that of CO₂). Part 3 comprises three measurements: the inner plane (containment directly at the working opening), the outer plane and the robustness test. In the robustness test a standardised plate (1900 × 400 × 20 mm) moves past the cupboard at 1 m/s and simulates a person walking briskly by – this checks whether the cupboard remains tight even when the airflow is disturbed. In operation, the on-site measurement to Part 4 checks function on the installed unit." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How often must a fume cupboard be tested?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Under TRGS 526 (Section 7.3) and the substantively identical DGUV Information 213-850, fume cupboards must be maintained regularly, their function tested and the result documented – at least once a year by a competent person. Overarching this, the Hazardous Substances Ordinance requires the employer to check the effectiveness of technical protective measures at least every third year; type, scope and test intervals are set by the employer on the basis of the risk assessment under their own responsibility. Items tested include face velocity or volume flow, the function of the airflow monitor and alarm, the sash mechanism and the physical condition. On VAV cupboards to Part 6 the function of the control loop must also be tested annually. This test is an operator duty – we deliver the cupboard ready for use; the operator organises the recurring test." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What types of fume cupboard are there?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The most common type is the bench-mounted (standing) cupboard with a work surface at about 900 mm height, available in the grid widths 1,200, 1,500 and 1,800 mm, with a typical volume flow of around 270 to 400 m³/h per linear metre. The low-level cupboard has a lowered work surface (approx. 500 mm) and therefore more internal height for tall apparatus such as distillation set-ups. The walk-in cupboard has the work surface at floor level and is intended for large-volume plant and pilot set-ups. In addition there are pass-through cupboards and special types for aggressive media: hydrofluoric-acid cupboards (HF-resistant lining), washable perchloric-acid cupboards with a rinse system against explosive perchlorate deposits, and cupboards for radioactive substances. Which type is appropriate depends on substances, apparatus and working method – in the container laboratory we select type, size and number to suit the layout and the available extract capacity." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why is recirculation of laboratory extract air not permitted?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Because the laboratory handles hazardous substances whose concentration in the extract air cannot be reliably cleaned to harmless levels. Laboratory extract from fume cupboards must therefore always be discharged to atmosphere – recirculation into the room is not permitted where hazardous substances are present. The standard is discharge over the roof (vertical discharge): thermal buoyancy and the vertical arrangement dilute the extract sufficiently and prevent re-entry into windows, doors or supply-air openings. Extract must also run continuously so that nothing escapes outside operating hours. Under DIN 1946-7 the room-air plant is to be designed so that an extract volume flow of at least 25 m³/h per square metre of laboratory floor area is not undershot – and correspondingly more when working with very hazardous substances." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How do you reduce the energy consumption of a fume cupboard?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Fume cupboards are the largest energy consumers in the laboratory – a single cupboard can use up to 3.5 times the energy of an average detached house, because air is constantly extracted and the incoming supply air must be conditioned (heated, cooled). The most effective lever is a VAV cupboard (Variable Air Volume) to DIN EN 14175-6: it modulates the extract volume flow according to sash position. With the sash closed, volume flow can be reduced by about 50 % relative to the open state; compared with a constantly running CAV cupboard, energy savings of up to 40 % are possible, and noise levels also fall. Heat recovery in the central ventilation plant and the simplest measure of all also help: close the sash consistently when not working at the cupboard. In the compact container laboratory with limited plant space, VAV is particularly worthwhile." } }
  ]
};
import RelatedArticles from '../../../components/RelatedArticles.astro';
